## The Absolute Defense Framework (Zero-Trust)
To ensure an enterprise is secure against the entire known spectrum of vulnerability chains, modern architecture relies on four absolute guardrails:

   1. Memory-Safe Languages & Hardware isolation: Moving critical financial kernels to memory-safe languages (like Rust) and utilizing hardware-level enclave technologies (like AWS Nitro Enclaves or Intel SGX) to completely isolate live transaction RAM from Rowhammer and kernel exploits.
   2. Cryptographic Routing Validation: Enforcing BGP Route Origin Validation (RPKI) to prevent internet-level traffic redirection and completely phasing out SMS-based 2FA in favor of hardware security keys (FIDO2/WebAuthn).
   3. Immutable CI/CD Pipelines & Dependency Pinning: Utilizing strict cryptographic hash verification (lock files) for every single external piece of code and ensuring production web environments have zero access to download unvetted public updates dynamically.
   4. Atomic, Signed Internal APIs: Requiring that even if an attacker gains RCE inside a web server, internal banking cores will strictly reject any payout API command unless it is signed by an independent, time-locked cryptographic key held by a separate isolated service.

